                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Note: I was provided with a key by the developer.[/h1]\n\nIf you don't want to read this review, you can basically hear me read it out loud here, with a little bit of gameplay footage:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/TkDqDLHIta4\n\nAqua Fish (as opposed to what kind of fish?) is a casual Match 3 game where you swipe to make combos and complete basic tasks to progress through the 100 levels. Objectives include things such as getting a certain score, collecting special items by dropping them to the bottom, collecting a set number of different items, clearing blocks and other simple tasks. You build up combos and make special pieces by matching higher numbers of various assorted sea-side items, but there's nothing new or different in the gameplay to every other casual Match 3 game, a great deal of which are free to play.\n\nFor achieving these goals you are rewarded with dollars, which you can spend in the store on buying fish and decorating your aquarium. This is quite relaxing, but ultimately ends up being quite limited as there isn't that much to buy.\n\n[h1]Pros:[/h1]\n[list]\n[*] Relaxing Gameplay\n[*] Bright and attractive graphics\n[*] Simple mechanics that anyone can play, making it ideal for young family members.\n[/list]\n\n[h1]Cons:[/h1]\n[list]\n[*] Animations are very slow\n[*] Combos don't feel very satisfying\n[*] Money and items you can buy are quite limited\n[*] Almost identical to other games on the mobile market\n[*] Limited lives, time-gating and in-game adverts in buy to play game.\n[/list]\nSo, onto that last point... This game has 5 lives. If you fail a level, you need to wait 30 minutes for your life to regenerate. If you lose all 5 lives, you can no longer play the game. You can of course watch an in-game advert to regain a life instantly.\n\nThis is not a free to play game. This is a full price full-release buy in game at £1.99 that still time gates you and forces you to watch adverts in order to play the game you've paid for. \n\nEven without in-game ads, this game is a clone of Playrix's mobile game \"Fishdom\". It brings nothing new to the table, but I could have given it an average recommendation for those who enjoy casual and relaxing gameplay. With the addition of freemium mobile elements in a buy to play game, it's getting a massive thumbs down from me, especially since this aspect is mentioned absolutely no where on the store page.\n\n[h1]Lose a life watch an advert is not an acceptable in-game strategy for full buy to play games.[/h1]",
